				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/110/236/949/771/783/169/original/b24293c1c7d961e3.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2023-08-24T23:13:35.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Perhaps I'm too cynical, but it seems to me that the focus shifts to enterprise whenever a technology is past the peak of its adoption curve. This maximizes profit during the long tail of usage by users that are more locked into the technology (usually big orgs that can't turn on a dime). In this case, it's not really about Gatsby, but about React more broadly, and where people perceive it to be in the tech cycle.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
